Run from the repository root, once for each real org.json implementation:

  python3 tests/gecko-timezone-routing-host/run.py --java-home /path/to/jdk17 --json-jar work/ux-build-deps/json-20240303.jar --output /tmp/timezone-routing-desktop.json
  python3 tests/gecko-timezone-routing-host/run.py --java-home /path/to/jdk17 --json-jar work/ux-build-deps/android-json.jar --output /tmp/timezone-routing-android-json.json

The runner extracts complete current production GeckoWebView methods for timezone
session admission, gate retirement/completion, native read/apply dispatch, SDK
window preparation, queued URI loading, loadUrl, restoreCheckpoint and the actual
onLoadRequest delegate. It also extracts PocketSessionIdentity.id and compiles
the unchanged BrowserBridgeRequests, JSON helpers and public URL policies.
Explicit intent claim/has/clear and the actual resumeProtectedLoads retry method
are also extracted. No older gate tests or assertions have been removed.
Extraction hashes and generated sources/commands/logs accompany each report.
Inputs must remain unchanged throughout the run. No existing test is edited.

Browser SDK types are renamed SessionBoundary, ResultBoundary, StateBoundary and
PortBoundary. These are JVM doubles, never fake org.mozilla SDK classes. The
controller is an explicit scripted app boundary: it records exact object/id/epoch
and supplies allow/deny; the real controller/store have independent tests. The
metadata status formatter, app owner/route/protection/draft policy, SDK serialized
state, Handler virtual clock and writer executor are also declared boundaries.

Behavioral coverage includes first URL and startup/checkpoint restoration waiting
for both SDK window readiness and controller permission, a five-second readiness
timeout reaching unready NativeAccess (no invented ACK), scripted default-system
fallback versus deny, stale object/epoch/port/retirement/disconnect callbacks,
duplicate first-navigation replay, latest queued target, filtered checkpoint
callback fences, exact read/apply sessionId/token/id and real per-port callback
correlation. Popup tests exercise actual timeZoneAllows/prepareTimeZone: unknown
objects fail, default-system delegates to controller popup policy, custom-zone
popups fail without starting an unopened SDK readiness cycle.

Each negative control separately compiles and passes its pristine focused case,
then removes one actual production condition and must fail the named assertion.
Controls cover first load/restore/navigation gates, gate-ready/controller checks,
retired callback ownership, SDK-window session/port fences, navigation/restore
epochs/current object and outbound/reply port fences. Compile failures, unrelated
assertions or mutant success are failures of the runner.
The after-writer control retires the captured native port between serialization
and handler delivery, requires a second SDK/controller readiness cycle, and
rejects removal of the actual post-worker timezone recheck.

Eight explicit-intent interleavings run twice with swapped target URLs: explicit
load then old native reload; old native reload then explicit load; explicit SDK
restore then old native reload; old native reload then restore; load then restore;
restore then load; two restores at the same epoch (both pending gate and writer);
old filtered writer then a new explicit load. SDK boundary load/restore events
must contain only the newest intended navigation. The actual protection retry is
executed after restore to expose a stale main URL that would otherwise revive.
Four additional precise mutants delete native-reload suppression while an
explicit intent is pending, native-deferred URL supersession, restore clearing
the old main URL, or the writer's exact-intent ownership check.

This executes app Java control flow on a host JVM. It does not execute Android,
GeckoView, native BrowsingContext timezone setters, the real controller/store,
Date/Intl, workers or network traffic; it makes no native readback or device claim.
